> The vertices of a triangle are at points (0, 0), (-1, 1), and (3, 3). What is the area of the triangle?
[0mLet the vertices of the triangle be $A(0,0)$, $B(-1,1)$, and $C(3,3)$.
We can use the formula for the area of a triangle given the coordinates of its vertices:
Area = $\frac{1}{2} |x_1(y_2 - y_3) + x_2(y_3 - y_1) + x_3(y_1 - y_2)|$
where $(x_1, y_1) = (0,0)$, $(x_2, y_2) = (-1,1)$, and $(x_3, y_3) = (3,3)$.
Plugging in the coordinates:
Area = $\frac{1}{2} |0(1-3) + (-1)(3-0) + 3(0-1)|$
Area = $\frac{1}{2} |0(-2) - 1(3) + 3(-1)|$
Area = $\frac{1}{2} |0 - 3 - 3|$
Area = $\frac{1}{2} |-6|$
Area = $\frac{1}{2} (6)$
Area = 3

Alternatively, we can use the determinant method:
Area = $\frac{1}{2} |(x_1(y_2 - y_3) + x_2(y_3 - y_1) + x_3(y_1 - y_2))|$
Area = $\frac{1}{2} |(0(1-3) + (-1)(3-0) + 3(0-1))|$
Area = $\frac{1}{2} |(0 - 3 - 3)|$
Area = $\frac{1}{2} |-6| = \frac{1}{2}(6) = 3$

Another method is to use the determinant of a matrix:
Area = $\frac{1}{2} \left| \begin{vmatrix} x_1 & y_1 & 1 \\ x_2 & y_2 & 1 \\ x_3 & y_3 & 1 \end{vmatrix} \right| = \frac{1}{2} \left| \begin{vmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\ 3 & 3 & 1 \end{vmatrix} \right|$
We can expand the determinant along the first row:
Area = $\frac{1}{2} | 1 \cdot \begin{vmatrix} -1 & 1 \\ 3 & 3 \end{vmatrix} | = \frac{1}{2} | (-1)(3) - (1)(3) |$
Area = $\frac{1}{2} | -3 - 3 | = \frac{1}{2} | -6 | = \frac{1}{2} (6) = 3$

We can also use the shoelace formula:
Area = $\frac{1}{2} |(x_1 y_2 + x_2 y_3 + x_3 y_1) - (x_2 y_1 + x_3 y_2 + x_1 y_3)|$
Area = $\frac{1}{2} |(0 \cdot 1 + (-1) \cdot 3 + 3 \cdot 0) - ((-1) \cdot 0 + 3 \cdot 1 + 0 \cdot 3)|$
Area = $\frac{1}{2} |(0 - 3 + 0) - (0 + 3 + 0)|$
Area = $\frac{1}{2} |(-3) - (3)| = \frac{1}{2} |-6| = \frac{1}{2} (6) = 3$

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{3}$
